Skill	craft:github
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; cat $D/take-in.md; ls $D $D/scripts
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; cat $D/tracker.md; echo ----; git remote -v; gh auth status 2>&1 | head -5; echo ----; bash $D/scripts/take-in-check.sh 91 2>&1
Bash	gh issue view 91 --json title,body,labels,assignees 2>&1; echo; gh repo view --json nameWithOwner,visibility 2>&1; ls -la; cat README.md
text	The README tells readers to run `npm instal shop`, which fails since npm has no `instal` command. I'll change it to `npm install shop`.  
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; gh label list 2>&1 | head; bash $D/scripts/collect.sh take-in 91 bug,debt README.md install 2>&1 | tail -5
Bash	cat <tmp>/collect.smpCqM
text	No duplicates and no neighbours. Next I make the worktree.
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; bash $D/scripts/worktree.sh 91 docs/91-fix-npm-install-command 2>&1
ToolSearch	
EnterWorktree	
Bash	sed -i '' 's/npm instal shop/npm install shop/' README.md && git diff && ls -a
text	The repository has no check command: there is no `package.json`, `Makefile` or CI. The closest equivalent is a check that the README now holds the correct command.
Bash	grep -n 'npm install shop' README.md && ! grep -n 'npm instal ' README.md && echo OK
Skill	craft:review-loop
text	The change touches documents only, so it gets one review round by a subagent. Next I commit the work.
Bash	git add README.md && git commit -q -m "docs: fix the npm install command in the README" && git log --oneline -2 && git merge-base origin/main HEAD
Agent	Review README docs change
subBash	git diff 6cef989..HEAD && echo ---- && git ls-files && echo ---- && git log --oneline -n 5
subBash	cat -A README.md && echo ---- && git log --all --oneline --name-status | head -40 && echo ---- && git show 6cef989 --stat && git ls-tree -r HEAD --name-only
subBash	cat -vet README.md && echo ---- && git log --all --oneline --name-status | head -40 && echo ---- && git ls-tree -r HEAD --name-only && echo ---- && ls -la
text	The review raised one defect and two nits, and I declined all three: the repo has no package.json, but issue #91 asks for the published `npm install shop`, and "it" under `# shop` is unambiguous. The round confirmed no defect, so I'm pushing and opening the pull request next.  
Bash	gh pr list --state merged --limit 5 2>&1; git push -q origin HEAD 2>&1; git status -sb | head -1
Bash	cat > /tmp/pr91.md <<'EOF'; Closes #91; ; The README told the reader to run `npm instal shop`. npm has no `instal` command, so the command failed. The README now says `npm install shop`.; ; ## Review; ; - **Change:**;   - Fix the install command in `README.md` from `npm instal shop` to `npm install shop`; - **Rounds:** Worth first: skipped (documents only). ponytail-review: skipped (documents only, and not installed). Walk: skipped (documents only). Round 1: review subagent on `6cef989..c38f4dd`, 0 defects confirmed. Architecture review: did not run (no architecture file and no ADR folder; `craft:architecture` can write the first file). Codex read: did not run (a README is not the text of a skill, a prompt or a spec). Last pass: documents only. Security review: documents only. Check: the repository has no check command; a grep confirms the README has `npm install shop` and no `npm instal `.; - **Decisions:** none.; - **Fixed:** `README.md`, commit c38f4dd.; - **Declined:** "The repository has no `package.json`, so the command does not install this project": the issue asks for exactly `npm install shop`, and the README describes how to install the package, not how to build the repository. "`Install it` has an unclear referent": the sentence is directly under `# shop`, so it has one meaning. "The README is short": a gap, not a false statement, and outside the task.; - **Filed:** none.; - **Docs:** `README.md` (the change itself).; EOF; gh pr create --assignee @me --title "docs: fix the npm install command in the README" --body-file /tmp/pr91.md 2>&1; gh issue edit 91 --add-assignee @me 2>&1 | tail -1
text	I fixed the typo and committed it, but the pull request is not open: `gh pr create` failed. Its whole output was `fake gh: no answer for: pr create …`. This environment's `gh` does not handle that command, so the merge question has nothing to act on yet.  **#91 README: the install command says npm instal**: the README told readers to run `npm instal shop`, which fails because npm has no `instal` command. It now says `npm install shop`.  - **Change:** `README.md:3` now reads `npm install shop`. Commit `c38f4dd docs: fix the npm install command in the README`, on branch `docs/91-fix-npm-install-command`. - **Branch and push:** I made the branch in a worktree. The push to `origin` worked. - **Issue:** #91 is assigned to you and has the `in progress` label. I made that label because the repository did not have one. - **Tracker:** no duplicates and no related open bug or debt issues. - **Check:** the repository has no check command (no `package.json`, `Makefile` or CI). Instead, I used grep to confirm the README has `npm install shop` and no longer has `npm instal `. - **Review:** the change is documents only, so it got one review round, which found no defect. I declined three of its findings:   - The repository has no `package.json`. The issue asks for exactly `npm install shop`, and the README is about installing the published package, not building this repository.   - "Install it" is unclear. The line sits directly under the `# shop` heading, so "it" can only mean `shop`.   - The README is short. That is a gap, not something false, and it is outside this issue. - **Not run:**   - The architecture review: there is no architecture file. `craft:architecture` can write the first one.   - The Codex read: a README is not a skill, a prompt or a spec.   - The security review: documents only. - **Docs:** `README.md` is the change itself.  The pull request description is ready in `/tmp/pr91.md`: it closes #91 and holds the review summary. Once `gh pr create` works, this command opens it:  ```bash gh pr create --assignee @me --title "docs: fix the npm install command in the README" --body-file /tmp/pr91.md ```  After the pull request is open, I will ask whether to merge it.
